title: 16. Types that check for you (units, distinct types, `requires`, typestate)
nav: Learn
order: 1600
template: docs
---
<p>Most production bugs are not exotic: a user id passed where an order id was expected, a zero where the code assumed a positive number, a parcel shipped twice. Four ways to make the compiler reject such programs, from cheapest to most precise.</p>

<h2 id="distinct">Distinct types: same number, different meaning</h2>
<p>An <code>i64</code> is an <code>i64</code> whether it is a user id, an order id or a count of bananas. A <em>distinct type</em> gives that representation a new identity:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct UserId = i64</code></pre>
<p>Build one with the type's name, <code>UserId(7)</code>, and read the number with <code>.value</code>; nothing converts silently in either direction.</p>
<example name="book-16-distinct"/>
<p>Both ids hold 7, and <code>same number: true</code> shows raw values compare; you cannot pass one where the other is expected.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct UserId = i64
type distinct OrderId = i64

string describe(UserId u) =&gt; $"user {u.value}"

void main() uses console {
    let o = OrderId(7)
    print(describe(o))
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `UserId`, found `OrderId`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> mix-ids.byn:8:20

<span class="gut">  8 │</span>     print(describe(<span class="hl">o</span>))
<span class="gut">    │</span>                    <span class="hl">^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>mix-ids.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>A bare number does not convert either; the fix-it wraps it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct UserId = i64

void main() {
    let bad: UserId = 0
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3204:</span> `0` is not a `UserId`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> raw-int.byn:4:23

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     let bad: UserId = <span class="hl">0</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>                       <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> wrap it: `UserId(0)`
<span class="gut">    │ 4 │</span>     let bad: UserId = <span class="add">UserId(0)</span>
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                       <span class="add">+++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3204</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>raw-int.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Arithmetic and comparison across a distinct type are closed off: <code>u + u</code> and <code>u &lt; v</code> are BYN2208, a cast <code>i64(u)</code> is BYN2206, and <code>{u}</code> in an interpolation is BYN2225 because the type has no text form. Go through <code>.value</code>, so each place where an id turns into a plain number stays visible in the source.</p>

<h2 id="requires">Values that must satisfy a rule</h2>
<p>Add <code>requires</code> to a distinct type and its constructor checks the rule against <code>it</code>, the value being checked.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Grams = i64 requires it &gt; 0</code></pre>
<p>A constant argument is checked while compiling:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Grams = i64 requires it &gt; 0

void main() {
    let g = Grams(0)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2321:</span> `0` violates `requires it &gt; 0` of `Grams`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> zero-grams.byn:4:19

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     let g = Grams(<span class="hl">0</span>)
<span class="gut">    │</span>                   <span class="hl">^ fails the contract</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> pass a value that meets `requires it &gt; 0`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2321</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>zero-grams.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>When the argument is known only at run time, the check can fail: the constructor fails with <code>ValidationError&lt;Grams&gt;</code>, which the caller must handle like any other error. Not handling it is BYN2602:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Grams = i64 requires it &gt; 0

Grams make(i64 raw) fails ValidationError&lt;Grams&gt; {
    return Grams(raw)
}

void main() fails ValidationError&lt;Grams&gt; {
    let g = make(5)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `Grams` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> no-try.byn:4:12

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     return <span class="hl">Grams(raw)</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>            <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
<span class="gut">    │ 4 │</span>     return <span class="add">try </span>Grams(raw)
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>            <span class="add">++++</span>

<span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `make` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> no-try.byn:8:13

<span class="gut">  8 │</span>     let g = <span class="hl">make(5)</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
<span class="gut">    │ 8 │</span>     let g = <span class="add">try </span>make(5)
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>             <span class="add">++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2602</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>no-try.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Use <code>try</code> to pass the error on, or <code>catch</code> to handle it: <code>parse</code> in <code>book-16-distinct</code> does the first, <code>main</code> the second. <code>parse(40) catch Grams(1)</code> gives a fallback; <code>catch switch { .invalid e =&gt; … }</code> picks a branch, which the call with 0 took, so <code>bad</code> is 2. A <code>Grams</code> is then known positive: checked once, at the door.</p>
<p>The same <code>requires</code> clause applies to a struct's fields, and <code>expects</code> to a function's arguments; <a href="features.html">What makes Bayan different</a> covers both.</p>

<h2 id="typestate">Typestate: the order of operations in the type</h2>
<p>Bugs come from calling the right method at the wrong time: sending a parcel twice, reading a signature before delivery. A <em>typestate</em> struct lists its states and the methods between them; the compiler tracks each variable's state.</p>
<example name="book-16-typestate"/>
<p>The <code>states</code> block lists <code>packed</code>, <code>sent</code> and <code>delivered</code>; a state can carry fields of its own, such as <code>carrier</code>. <code>send</code> takes <code>ref this is .packed</code>, so only a packed parcel may call it: <code>becomes .sent</code> says where it ends up, and <code>become .sent(carrier: carrier)</code> moves it. <code>receipt</code> takes <code>Parcel.delivered p</code>, no other state will do; after <code>deliver</code>, <code>main</code> reads <code>p.carrier</code>, a delivered-state field.</p>
<p>The output is <code>parcel 1 signed by Umar</code>, then <code>DHL</code>. Now send twice:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Id = i64

struct Parcel {
    Id id
    states {
        packed
        sent(string carrier)
    }

    void send(ref this is .packed, by string carrier) becomes .sent {
        become .sent(carrier: carrier)
    }
}

void main() uses console {
    var p = Parcel.packed { id: Id(1) }
    p.send(by: "DHL")
    p.send(by: "UPS")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7001:</span> `send` needs `p` to be `.packed`, but it is `Parcel.sent`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> wrong-state.byn:18:5

<span class="gut">  18 │</span>     <span class="hl">p</span>.send(by: "UPS")
<span class="gut">     │</span>     <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> check the state with `is` or `switch` first

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN7001</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>wrong-state.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>After the first <code>send</code>, <code>p</code> is <code>.sent</code>; the second call needs <code>.packed</code>, so it is rejected: a parcel cannot ship twice. State fields are protected too: reading a one-state field without proving the variable is in that state is an error.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Parcel {
    states {
        packed
        sent(string carrier)
    }
}

void main() uses console {
    let p = Parcel.packed {}
    print(p.carrier)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7002:</span> `carrier` is a payload of `.sent`; `p` is not known to be `.sent`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> payload-early.byn:10:11

<span class="gut">  10 │</span>     print(<span class="hl">p.carrier</span>)
<span class="gut">     │</span>           <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> match `.sent` with `is` or `switch` before reading `carrier`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN7002</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>payload-early.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Other typestate errors: BYN7003, a <code>become</code> in a method without <code>becomes</code>; BYN7004, a <code>switch</code> over the states that misses one; BYN7005, a struct literal that names no state, such as <code>Parcel {…}</code> for <code>Parcel.packed {…}</code>.</p>

<h2 id="units">Units of measure</h2>
<note>Planned, not in the compiler yet: a <code>unit</code> declaration, so <code>3.0 m</code> and <code>2.0 s</code> carry their dimension, adding metres to seconds is a compile error, and <code>m / s</code> gives a speed. The compiler does not parse <code>unit</code> today; until then, use a distinct type per quantity, with the checks above.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">// planned
unit m
unit s
let d = 100.0 m
let t = 9.58 s
let v = d / t        // m / s</code></pre>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. <code>describe(o)</code> takes a <code>UserId</code> and is given an <code>OrderId</code> holding the same number. What happens?
<ol type="a"><li>It works, since both are <code>i64</code>.</li><li>BYN2202, expected <code>UserId</code>, found <code>OrderId</code>.</li><li>It panics at run time.</li></ol></summary>
<p>b. Same representation, separate identity, so (a) is wrong; checked at compile time, so (c) is wrong. The working program shows <code>u.value == o.value</code> as <code>true</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. What does <code>Grams(0)</code> do when <code>Grams</code> requires <code>it &gt; 0</code>?</summary>
<p>A compile error, BYN2321, because the constant breaks the rule; not a run-time failure. A run-time value instead fails with <code>ValidationError&lt;Grams&gt;</code> and needs <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. In <code>book-16-distinct</code>, why is <code>bad</code> equal to 2?</summary>
<p><code>parse(0)</code> fails the <code>requires</code> check, so its error reaches the <code>catch switch</code>, whose <code>.invalid</code> branch gives <code>Grams(2)</code>. <code>ok</code> is 40 because 40 passed and the fallback was unused.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>4. Why does the second <code>p.send(by: "UPS")</code> not compile?</summary>
<p>The first <code>send</code> moved <code>p</code> to <code>.sent</code>, and <code>send</code> needs <code>.packed</code> (BYN7001); <code>p</code> is still valid, so it is no move error. It is no run-time check either: the program is rejected before it runs.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>5. Can you declare <code>unit m</code> today?</summary>
<p>No, units are planned. Use <code>type distinct</code> per quantity.</p></details>

